  "transcript": "# British film and high-end television follow-up\n\n## Where the sector stands now\n> Box office has recovered to pre-COVID levels, but UK production faces a dip amid fiercer international competition.\n\nChair: Harriet, how is the sector now compared with our 2025 report?\nHarriet Finney: Box office is back to around 100 million admissions — driven by a few big films — so cinema-going recovered strongly this year.\nHarriet Finney: But production is down: globally fewer commissions and strong competition from other countries; the UK’s production market share is still high but pressured.\nPete Johnson: Long-term demand for audiovisual content is high, but other countries have aggressively increased production support — Spain, for example, boosted spend dramatically.\nClaire Mundell: Financing remains the critical problem: mid‑tier British cultural projects and regionally rooted stories struggle to find money.\n\n\n## Tax reliefs and where change is needed\n> The independent film tax relief helped make films that otherwise wouldn't exist, but gaps remain for mid‑tier TV and R&D support.\n\nDr Huq: Did the independent film tax credit (IFTC) deliver and is it at risk from the new US federal incentives?\nHarriet Finney: The IFTC has enabled many projects—about 400 certified so far—and we’ll publish a fuller impact assessment in 2027, but more data is needed.\nPete Johnson: It’s already helping, but we also need measures to crowd in risk finance and to improve distribution so films actually get seen.\nClaire Mundell: Mid‑budget TV (roughly £1m–£3m per hour) and development finance are badly squeezed; producers rely on tax credits and need R&D tax relief included.\nRt Hon Ian Murray MP: The reliefs put in place are a decision for the Treasury; DCMS is making the case and working with industry to build the strongest possible evidence.\n\n\n## Distribution, exhibition and the BFI's role\n> National Lottery funding supports small British stories, talent development and audiences; distribution and marketing remain weak links.\n\nGill German: If the Government reforms lottery distribution, what would be at risk?\nHarriet Finney: The lottery funds grassroots activity: small British films, talent programmes (like the BFI Film Academy) and audience work in 2,000 venues — many projects go unfunded now.\nPete Johnson: Even when films are made, they often lack marketing budgets and distribution pathways; the sector supports a distribution tax relief idea that could leverage VAT returns.\nLiz Jarvis: Are the Government tracking whether screen growth funds reach the domestic and independent sector?\nRt Hon Ian Murray MP: We use BFI and other data on GVA, workforce and skills to track impact; the Screen Growth Package and doubled global screen fund are designed to help.\nGeorgia Hutchinson: We’ve also put Creative Places Growth Fund money through mayoral authorities to match local priorities and catalyse region‑specific projects.\n\n\n## Freelancers, skills and regional production\n> Freelancers are essential but insecure; data gaps, apprenticeships and regional commissioning are urgent issues for workforce health.\n\nJo Stevens: What should the Freelance Champions prioritise for freelancers in film and TV?\nPete Johnson: Finding work is the immediate crisis: many freelancers consider leaving because of instability; other countries provide better safety nets for creative freelancers.\nClaire Mundell: Freelancers are the engine room. Without development funding and clearer commissioning plans, talent will leave and the pipeline will dry up.\nJo Stevens: Who collects the data needed to fix skills and levy issues?\nRt Hon Ian Murray MP: Multiple departments hold relevant data — DCMS, DfE, Treasury and DWP — and we’re supporting the Freelance Champions to pull it together with industry bodies like ScreenSkills.\nJo Stevens: What progress on apprenticeships and the levy for a freelance-heavy sector?\nRt Hon Ian Murray MP: We need shorter, flexible training and apprenticeship models that suit short-term productions; the jobs plan and skills audit are trying to address that.\nHarriet Finney: Good Work tools like WorkWise, CIISA and sector charity support are being scaled to help smaller employers create safer working environments for freelancers.\nGill German: If public broadcasters cut regional commissioning, what would you do to protect regional hubs?\nRt Hon Ian Murray MP: The charter renewal will push PSBs to support regional hubs; mayors and local Growth Fund grants also catalyse regional ecosystems rather than just competing with them.\nGeorgia Hutchinson: Local commissioning can grow adjacent industries — animation and VFX in a region can take on special effects for bigger shows, keeping skills local.\n\n\n## AI, copyright and protecting performers\n> Industry welcomes clearer rules and funding to experiment with AI, but wants stronger legal protection around consent, replicas and deepfakes.\n\nLiz Jarvis: Has progress been made to protect performers from unauthorised cloning, deepfakes and use of recorded performances to train AI?\nHarriet Finney: We welcomed Government moving away from a broad opt-out. BFI has invested £1.2m in R&D labs to help creators test tools and we’ll publish guidance next year.\nPete Johnson: Legal certainty around liabilities and where value sits is essential for investment in AI tools; industry engagement with the IPO is underway.\nDr Huq: ScreenSkills warns 80% of roles could be affected by AI but 70% had no training — will you meet unions and industry groups?\nRt Hon Ian Murray MP: Yes — we’ll meet the APPG and unions. We have four AI copyright workstreams on transparency, labelling, creator control and digital replicas.\nGeorgia Hutchinson: The consultation will distinguish between consented uses, like notable posthumous examples, and potentially exploitative uses of early‑career performers.\n\n\n## BBC, governance and platform contributions\n> The Government will press the BBC to protect regional commissioning and skills in the charter White Paper; a streamer levy is not currently planned.\n\nChair: How will the charter review ensure BBC impartiality, trust and stronger processes?\nRt Hon Ian Murray MP: The White Paper will target trust and impartiality, improving processes and culture so the BBC leads by example on editorial standards and workplace behaviour.\nAlison Hume: Streamers often buy broad rights and limit indies' ability to monetise IP — will you consider a streamer levy?\nRt Hon Ian Murray MP: A streamer levy has been ruled out for now; we favour a mix of tax reliefs, regional funds, PSB commissioning and better access to private finance to support indies.\nPete Johnson: Targeted growth capital and funds that value AV IP would help indies negotiate better and retain rights, rather than relying solely on streamers.\nChair: Any final messages?\nHarriet Finney: Keep pushing outstanding recommendations: protect lottery funding, support domestic stories, and optimise incentives so inward investment and home-grown production both thrive.\nClaire Mundell: Strengthen the BBC’s economic role in nations and regions, protect terms of trade for indies, and consider focused funds for at‑risk genres like children’s and regional drama.\nPete Johnson: This ecosystem is tightly linked: tax, distribution, training and access to finance must work together if the UK is to keep its edge.\nRt Hon Ian Murray MP: The sector is a national asset — fragile but strong. We’ll continue backing it through funding, the charter review, AI workstreams and regional investment.\n",
